  "culturalNote": "Pro patria (for the fatherland) was a central Roman concept. Dulce et decorum est pro patria mori ('It is sweet and fitting to die for the fatherland') from Horace encapsulates the Roman ideal of civic duty and sacrifice.",
  "readingPassage": {
    "latin": "Milites Romani e castris exeunt. Per silvam ad oppidum hostium ambulant. Trans flumen cum armis natant. Post proelium, sine hostibus ad castra redeunt. Pro Roma semper pugnant.",
    "translation": "The Roman soldiers go out from the camp. They walk through the forest to the town of the enemies. They swim across the river with their weapons. After the battle, without the enemies they return to the camp. They always fight for Rome.",
